quation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1061 15458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0e30d07-9c41-5c2c-839c-9e49ca64764c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1061 15459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "233132be-0fc4-52d1-9152-818b8c45b168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1061 15460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13e292aa-c881-5526-9904-6d174bbb856b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1061 15461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "634f203a-900b-5f9f-977b-e1bb2ab2c5ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1061 17016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b043ea3a-fd3a-5de4-b7ac-21a29627ca53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1065 3123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33bd96be-8ef8-525c-be19-8590b4893b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592