/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540000 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540000 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540000 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540000 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7cb7b4d-8e3a-52e0-b39c-57977a95c75f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9929bc33-2631-5ab2-871b-80ee305fc700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540075 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+5=3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =3 \\\\ x+5-5 & =3-5 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540100 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04bd31c0-ddc2-5b9c-ad07-d73251e35927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "128aaed4-1578-549e-bc3a-0ca44706efdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540140 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540140 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5a1efe66-123d-5aba-9cc1-514789a8cab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540140 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540075 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
